guile_cpp, vendored in TeXmacs
==============================

guile_cpp is GNU Guile 1.8.8 rewritten in C++ by PikachuHy: a tagged value
representation, a mark-and-sweep collector which scans the C stack
conservatively, a CEK evaluator and a bytecode compiler with its VM, and the
library of libguile 1.8 (numbers with their own bignums, strings, ports,
modules, GOOPS, SRFIs...). It boots the unmodified Scheme library of Guile
1.8.8 (ice-9/boot-9.scm...).

Origin:   https://github.com/PikachuHy/guile_cpp
Commit:   cd6c4b5c6df9ef22d8fc96190a0d879adaafee6a (27 November 2026)
Author:   PikachuHy <pikachuhy@163.com>
License:  pending with its author (guile_cpp is derived from Guile 1.8.8,
          LGPL 2.1 or later; the Scheme files of share/ keep their GNU
          notices). TeXmacs packages must not ship it until it is settled.

The interface of TeXmacs (the tmscm functions) is ../guile_cpp_tm.cpp and
../guile_cpp_tm.hpp. Configure TeXmacs with --with-scheme=guile_cpp (macOS
and Linux only: guile_cpp uses POSIX headers; the browser cannot scan its C
stack). The Scheme side is the one of Guile 1.8: TeXmacs/progs/init-guile.scm.

What is kept
------------

src/                the sources of libguile_cpp, without main.cpp (the guile
                    command line) and jit_arm64.cpp (see below)
include/guile_cpp/  its headers
share/guile/1.8/    the Scheme library (ice-9, oop, srfi), without the
                    Makefile.am of Guile; `make` copies it into
                    TeXmacs/boot/guile_cpp (target GUILE_CPP_DATA of
                    src/Makefile.in), where guile_cpp_tm.cpp finds it from
                    TEXMACS_PATH, so that the packages are relocatable

Not kept: the CMake build, the tests (tests/, test-suite/), the benchmarks,
the README of the project.

Building
--------

The sources are compiled by src/src/makefile.in as Objects/gcpp_<name>.o
(some names are those of sources of TeXmacs, list.cpp, so that this folder
is also left out of the vpath of the makefile), with the flags of TeXmacs:
C++17 and -O2 (guile_cpp itself is built with -O3 by its CMake). Only the
objects of src/Scheme/guile_cpp see the include folder: the rest of TeXmacs
includes include/guile_cpp/value.hpp alone, through guile_cpp_tm.hpp.

Changes to guile_cpp
--------------------

* C++17 instead of C++20: include/guile_cpp/compat.hpp replaces <bit>
  (gcpp_countr_zero, gcpp_countl_zero, gcpp_popcount, gcpp_bit_ceil, with
  the results of the standard functions for 0) and the attributes
  [[likely]] / [[unlikely]] of the if and while statements
  (GCPP_LIKELY / GCPP_UNLIKELY, with __builtin_expect).
* No JIT: jit_arm64.cpp (the ARM64 compiler of macOS) is not compiled; its
  calls in bytecode.cpp are under #ifdef GUILE_CPP_JIT, which TeXmacs never
  defines, and VM::jit_enabled is false by default.
* include/guile_cpp/value.hpp: the tagged values (SCM), the header of the
  heap objects (Obj, ObjTag), the pairs and is_pair, is_symbol, is_string,
  moved out of scm.hpp, which includes it.
* load.cpp: the directories of the Scheme library are given at run time
  (set_data_dirs, called before VM::init), instead of the paths compiled in
  by the CMake build (GUILE_CPP_PKGDATA_DIR...), which remain the defaults
  when they are defined.
* No std::function: SubrFn (the builtins) is a function pointer, or for a
  lambda which captures a copy of it made with tm_new, shared by reference
  counting (SubrClosure); the callbacks of the collector and of the smob
  types are function pointers with a data pointer (Marker); the recursive
  local lambdas take themselves as first argument.
* The containers of TeXmacs where the change was local: the tables of the
  VM are hashmaps (symbols, extensions, fast builtins, GOOPS cmethods,
  source positions), with hash (SCM) in scm.hpp; copy-tree uses a hashset;
  the zombies of guardians, the stacks of the bytecode VM (with a raw
  pointer to the frames, frame_data, as fast as std::vector on tak) are
  arrays; the bindings of environments are TeXmacs pairs; bigint.cpp owns
  its scratch chunks with tm_new_array instead of unique_ptr.

Strings and vectors
-------------------

The strings and vectors of guile_cpp are TeXmacs strings and arrays
(std::string, std::string_view, std::vector and the shared_ptr<StringBuf>
of the Scheme strings are gone). include/guile_cpp/tm_string.hpp has the
helpers for the operations of std::string and std::vector which TeXmacs
writes otherwise (str_append, str_sub, str_find, gcpp_data, gcpp_clear,
gcpp_resize, array_from, array_fill, array_splice...), and begin / end
for the range-for loops. The views of std::string_view are a pointer and a
length: StringObj::chars () and len, view_sub, view_find.

A copy of a TeXmacs string or array shares its characters or elements
(there is no copy on write), where a copy of a std::string or std::vector
had its own. Every site where a value is copied and then changed, or kept
by an object which must own it, was checked:

copy () added (the value is changed later, or must be owned):
* SmallScmVec (the values of the frames of the evaluator): its copy
  constructor and assignment copy the array; eval.cpp copies cf->vals and
  then adds to the copy (4 places).
* make-dynamic-state (fluids.cpp): the values of the parent state, which
  fluid-set! changes.
* vector-copy (vectors.cpp): the elements (they were shared).
* sort of a vector (sort.cpp): the elements, sorted in place.
* get_array_info (arrays.cpp): the dimensions of the array.
* make_input_string_port (ports.cpp): the characters (seek, truncate).
* VM::make_string (string): the characters, as std::string did;
  make_symbol and make_uninterned_symbol: the name of a new symbol (a
  lookup shares the name: no allocation).
* the binding: string_to_tmscm and tmscm_to_string copy once (as before),
  tmscm_to_symbol copies the name (the caller may change its string).

No copy (checked):
* make_string_owned (no copy) only for strings built locally and not
  kept (string-append, string-join, string-pad, make-string, reader
  tokens, read-line, numbers...: 21 places, the std::move of guile_cpp).
* shared substrings share the characters of their string, by design.
* the name of a symbol is shared by the symbol table (key) and the
  symbol, and never changed; the names of subrs, the file names and modes
  of ports, the patterns of regexps, the layouts of structs (struct.cpp,
  gc.cpp, goops.cpp, arrays.cpp) and the fields of socket addresses are
  only read.
* no function changes a string or array parameter in place (all were
  checked); assignments from locals which are no longer used replace the
  swaps of std::vector.
* clearing gives a new empty value (gcpp_clear), never resizes a value
  which may be shared.
* the views of the characters of a Scheme string stay valid: a StringObj
  never reallocates its characters (string-set! writes in place).

Speed: a TeXmacs array resizes its storage at each change of its size for
small sizes, and A and N take the array by value (its count of references
changes at each call). Measured on tak and the suites, the stacks which
grow and shrink at each step got a count of their own over an array which
never shrinks (the worklist of the collector, the active VM invocations),
and the hot pointers of the bytecode VM are kept (the stacks, the code,
the globals and the constants of the running bytecode); the string
operations read the characters in place (StringObj::chars, sub) instead
of copying the whole string.

Other fixes of guile_cpp
------------------------

* The VM stack can be reallocated by a nested run of the VM: the subrs
  (not the glue routines of TeXmacs, raw_fn, which read their arguments
  before running anything) get a copy of their arguments
  (call_subr_copied, bytecode.cpp), setup_closure_args copies its
  arguments when the stack would grow, and GLOBAL_REF / GLOBAL_SET reload
  the stack pointer after sym2var (a module binder runs Scheme).
* dlerror is read once (dynamic-link of a missing file crashed).

Still from the standard library
-------------------------------

<algorithm> (std::sort and std::stable_sort with comparators, std::min /
std::max on size_t and int64_t, std::reverse, std::find,
std::binary_search), std::move / std::forward / std::swap, <complex>,
<cmath>, <chrono> (GC times), <random> (std::mt19937, std::random_device),
<limits>, <type_traits>, <initializer_list>, std::exception (the binding
catches it), and the C library (<cstring>, <cstdio>, <cctype>...).
